ther than on every function call to avoid unnecessary recompilation, so I need to refactor that approach.\n\nNow thinking through the error handling in `execBinary` more carefully—when the context deadline expires, `cmd.Run()` will return an error and the process gets killed. I should check `ctx.Err()` after `cmd.Run()` fails to properly distinguish a timeout from other errors, since `ctx.Err()` will be set to `context.DeadlineExceeded` in that case.\n\nRefining the error handling logic...\n\nI need to move the regex compilation to the package level since the TimestampNormalizer function could be called repeatedly. Also, I'm reconsidering the Timeout field—the PRD explicitly lists the struct fields in R1.1 without mentioning Timeout, but R2.3 requires configurable timeout support, so I need to figure out how to reconcile these requirements without deviating from the spec. I've settled on adding a Timeout field to the DiffTest struct since R2.3 requires per-test-case timeout configurability, and that's the most natural place for it despite R1.1 listing specific fields. Now I'm finalizing the implementation and realizing I should import the `strings` package to use `strings.HasPrefix` for the environment variable matching, especially since I'm already using `strings.Join` elsewhere. One more thing to verify—R3.5 specifies what the failure message should include, so I need to make sure that's properly formatted.\n\nLooking through the requirements, my implementation handles the key constraints: R3.6 uses `t.Run` for named subtests, R2.4 ensures no output during passing tests, R2.6 correctly applies `LC_ALL=C` as a base environment that user settings can override, and R4.2 needs the TimestampNormalizer to replace common strftime patterns with a placeholder.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the file comparison logic—both binaries run in the same working directory, so if the reference binary writes files that the Go binary then overwrites, I'd be comparing against the Go binary's output. I need to verify this matches the requirement that ExpectedFiles should compare byte-for-byte against the reference binary's output. at the ExpectedFiles map being pre-specified by the test author rather than derived from the reference binary's actual output, which would avoid the file overwriting problem entirely. The test flow would be: run both binaries, compare their stdout/stderr/exit codes, then verify the files on disk match the pre-defined expected content.\n\nFor utilities that write to files, the test author needs to account for the fact that both binaries run in the same workdir—if they both write to the same file, the second will overwrite the first. So either the test setup needs to handle that, or the expectations need to reflect that reality. Let me just follow the spec as written and move forward with the implementation. I'm going in circles on the working directory issue—both binaries running in the same directory means the reference binary could modify files that the Go binary then reads, which is problematic for file-output testing. But the spec is clear that they share a workdir, and real test cases would be designed to handle this anyway. Let me just implement what's specified and move forward. I also need to verify that the `pkg/testutils/` directory exists before creating the file.\n\nWriting the differential testing harness... Defining the test structure... Tests executes each DiffTest as a named subtest via t.Run. For each test case, it runs both goBinary and refBinary with identical Args, Stdin, and Env, then compares stdout, stderr, and exit code byte-for-byte after normalization.\n\nNow I'm setting up the test environment—using the provided timeout or falling back to a default, creating a temporary directory if needed, and building the environment variables. Then I execute the reference binary first, followed by the Go binary, both with the same inputs and configuration.\n\nComparing the outputs... Still writing test validation... Writing helper functions... Finishing up the error handling for the command execution, then returning the result with stdout, stderr, and exit code. After that, I'm defining a function to apply a series of normalizer functions to data in sequence, and starting to set up some precompiled regex patterns for timestamp normalization. Writing the normalizer function...\n\nNow I'm verifying the acceptance criteria against what I've implemented—the package structure, exported types, and test execution logic all check out. I'm realizing I need to reconsider the order of regex replacements in the timestamp normalizer, since the simpler time pattern could potentially match parts of more complex timestamp formats. I'm confident these three common strftime formats cover what the requirements ask for, so I'm ready to implement this.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11856,"cache_read_input_tokens":35382,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11856},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"eeb80a62-f416-48e8-abb0-3af90791f644","uuid":"43cfa4b8-e186-4ab3-8135-b697b8d7357a"}
